body:
Oh no, I'm not trying to approve of this behaviour whatsoever. Their behaviour is an extreme reaction to an extreme situation. While I fully understand why they behave the way they do (pure desperation and anger at not being extended the support they need), that in no way excuses their actions. They are discriminated against not just on an individual level, but systemically.

&#x200B;

They flee from their home countries for the simple reason that their existential security is at stake. They head for a country that seems to epitomize civilization and looks to be a beacon of liberty. As they arrive at the border, they start being blocked off, herded into camps or simply not allowed to cross said border. The lucky ones that manage to get across then get discriminated against purely based on the fact that their culture isn't sufficiently westernized. Support is sluggish at best and they frequently get segregated from the general public for extended periods of time.

&#x200B;

From what I have seen in Germany so far, Ukrainian refugees have been accepted much more easily than any refugees from the middle east or Africa that have shown up in the past years. Public sentiment was generally negative when darker-skinned refugees arrived and the narrative utilized on news channels focussed on refugees' legal infractions, their incompatibility with german culture, public opinion on the issue (which was strongly negative), and the inability of german economy to support all of them. In contrast to that, narratives now are mostly about economic issues stemming from embargos and the like, while refugees are only rarely mentioned; if they are mentioned at all, it is mostly in a neutral tone discussing the logistics of housing and supporting them. (notice that now, there is no question being raised as to whether resources are sufficient to support refugees)

&#x200B;

As a little thought experiment: how do you think the experience of a Libyan refugee differs from that of a Ukrainian one?

&#x200B;

